+{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"tool_cYOLbUZu0xwaRs2XdMdMwgJQ","content":"1\t# Literature host interfaces · plan r6, amended at close\n2\t\n3\t**Status: r6 draft, 2026-11-23. Not approved; r6's own bytes are not\n4\treviewed.** The owner selected the full GND review panel (D14, ruled `(o)`),\n5\tand it ran twice. The first round read r4 (sha256\n6\t`6cadaa9134585b348d53ef50ef5c35638a3b0bb20c7c36726ac53039ac357cb6`): four of\n7\tsix reviewers succeeded, all asking for revision, with twelve ranked fixes\n8\t([r4 record](../../../reviews/literature-host-interfaces-r4-panel/README.md)).\n9\tr5 applied them, tagged `(p1)`. The second round read r5 (sha256\n10\t`c1a832378a2f303cdf1be8884ea61e745eca7f22e4fab19a449761d62b082c9b`): five of\n11\tsix succeeded, one approving and four asking for revision, with seven ranked\n12\tfixes ([r5 record](../../../reviews/literature-host-interfaces-r5-panel/README.md)).\n13\tr6 applies those, tagged `(p2)`. §11 maps every fix of both rounds to its\n14\tsites. The convergence rules allow no third panel round. The owner's rulings on D6, D9 and D10 (2026-11-22, r3) and on D14 and\n15\tD17 (2026-11-23, r4 and r5) are tagged `(o)`. r2 to r4 were never committed.\n16\tNo plan-gate record exists. The owner gates the plan before any spec is\n17\tapproved and before any code.\n18\t\n19\tThis plan covers [#216](https://github.com/vasiloglou/gnd-public/issues/216)\n20\tand [#215](https://github.com/vasiloglou/gnd-public/issues/215). It delivers\n21\tthe interface half of the \"host-scale reading and extraction\" carryover from\n22\tthe [literature-depth close](../../literature-depth/016-milestone-close.md).\n23\tThe run over a separately selected corpus and provider budget stays carried\n24\twith the literature-identity follow-up, as that close assigned it.\n25\t\n26\t**Provenance.** Outline r1 is the previous bytes of this file (sha256\n27\t`abefb05f7fa7c19d068182eeeb557efea36bbc89d555647d98ec350b1017bea0`, commit\n28\t`0cf94434`, merged in PR 219). Its phase IDs LH1–LH4 and decisions D1–D3 are\n29\tkept. Its reviews, `.gnd-planning/reviews/pr-219-r2/` and `pr-219-panel/`,\n30\tread the roadmap outline, not these bytes. The r4 panel's opinion covers r4's\n31\tbytes only, and the r5 panel's covers r5's; no sentence new in r6 carries a\n32\treview opinion. Every code citation was re-read on 2026-11-22 at `4ad0d216`,\n33\tand those r5 and r6 add or correct on 2026-11-23 at the same commit;\n34\t`src/gnd/examples/literature/` is unchanged since `0f39c392`, where the outline\n35\tcited it. The outline wrote the two module paths without `src/gnd/`; they are\n36\t`src/gnd/examples/literature/reading.py` and\n37\t`src/gnd/examples/literature/assertion_admission.py`. Below, `reading.py`,\n38\t`assertion_admission.py` and `depth.py` mean the files in that folder.\n39\t\n40\t## Goal and completion predicates\n41\t\n42\tA host with its own corpus and its own model calls the two literature examples\n43\tas library functions instead of reimplementing them. The examples stay\n44\texamples: neither is promoted to stable CLI (both issues say so).\n45\t\n46\t- **LH1.** `reading.py` has a public producer protocol. `run()` drives the six\n47\t  stages with a caller's producer over a caller's input root: its own\n48\t  documents, its own reading model with its own includes, no saved responses\n49\t  and no producer pin. Each of the fixture-only steps runs on caller data or\n50\t  is recorded as skipped. The run has a byte budget per document as well as\n51\t  the global one, and its caps are parameters. The responses and review\n52\t  receipts a live run produces are retained in its run folder.\n53\t- **LH2.** `replay()` re-derives a live LH1 run from its run folder and the\n54\t  caller's input root with zero producer calls and zero reviewer calls. It\n55\t  takes every setting from the run, and refuses a changed response, receipt or\n56\t  input.\n57\t- **LH3.** `assertion_admission.py` admits assertions of a caller-named entity\n58\t  in a caller's model. An assertion is identified by its own handle `(o)`.\n59\t  Every fact row attached to an admitted assertion is kept, whatever its fact\n60\t  type, together with the facts of the entities reached from it `(o)`. Absent\n61\t  optional facts are simply absent. An include root is admitted.\n62\t- **LH4.** Which values make a supported assertion admissible is caller data.\n63\t  A supported assertion that fails those rules has a status of its own. The\n64\t  target cap is a parameter.\n65\t\n66\tThe milestone is closed when each phase has its record and the close record\n67\tcites green focused lanes for LH1–LH4, the structure and inventory guards,\n68\tthe whole-repository `uv run ruff check`, as `project.md` requires `(p1)`,\n69\tthe replies drafted for #216 and #215, and the close review. Baseline, measured 2026-11-22 at `4ad0d216`\n70\twith gnd 0.4.0:\n71\t`uv run pytest -q tests/test_ld3_reading_workflow.py tests/test_ld4_revision.py\n72\ttests/test_ld5_verdict.py tests/test_li4_assertions.py tests/test_li5_lineage.py\n73\t--tb=short` gave `90 passed in 166.21s`; `uv run ruff check src tests tools`\n74\texits 0. The whole-repository `uv run ruff check` exited 1 at `4ad0d216`,\n75\twith all 198 findings in the scripts delivered from dev-tools (H25). The owner\n76\thad them fixed upstream: dev-tools PR 34, merged as `a29562a`. After\n77\t`devtools/sync.py apply` from that commit, the whole-repository lint exits 0\n78\tand the gate-guard and scaffold tests pass (measured 2026-11-23). The re-sync\n79\tis committed with this plan, so LH1 starts from a `main` where the whole\n80\trepository lints clean `(p1)`: the start condition in §3 is that commit, not\n81\t`4ad0d216` `(p2)`.\n82\t\n83\t## 0. Binding design rules\n84\t\n85\t- **R0.1 Formats stay.** The stage record (`stage-N.json`), the attempt rows\n86\t  and their three outcomes, and the coverage rows with their eight states are\n87\t  unchanged; #216 says they work as they are. `literature-rebind/1` belongs to\n88\t  `interop.py` and is not touched.\n89\t- **R0.2 Packaged bytes stay.** With no new argument, the three packaged\n90\t  reading run configurations (`full`, `--scenario unavailable` and\n91\t  `--budget-bytes 0`; `SCENARIOS` itself has two values, `reading.py:38`)\n92\t  `(p1)` and the packaged admission write the bytes they write today. The comparison goes through `tests/support/recorded_version.py`,\n93\t  because the reading run retains two fidelity reports that carry\n94\t  `gnd_version` (H11).\n95\t- **R0.3 Example status stays.** Public here means named in the example's\n96\t  README and covered by tests. No `gnd` subcommand is added, and the two\n97\t  module command lines keep every argument they have.\n98\t- **R0.4 One loop, one walk.** Reading: the caller route and the packaged\n99\t  route run the same stage loop; the packaged constants become default data\n100\t  for it, not a second copy of it (standing rule 4). Admission `(p1)`: the\n101\t  D10 ruling keeps the packaged model, whose `assertion` objectifies a fact\n102\t  type, out of the generic projection, so admission has two projections, the\n103\t  packaged one as it is today and the generic attached-and-followed one. They\n104\t  share everything else, each in one home: the snapshot and the\n105\t  `gnd judgments check` route (`_judgments`, `_verify_report_inputs`), the\n106\t  mapping from a target's verdicts to a support reason (the target half of\n107\t  `_support`), the admission-rule evaluator of LH4, and the record writer and\n108\t  publication. The packaged projection gains no feature of the generic one;\n109\t  it stops at the packaged shape, as it does today.\n110\t- **R0.5 A setting is retained evidence.** Every argument that shapes a caller\n111\t  run is written into the run. Replay reads settings from the run, never from\n112\t  its caller.\n113\t- **R0.6 Skipped is not passed.** A step the caller did not supply is recorded\n114\t  with the status `skipped`. No report presents it as qualified, as measured\n115\t  or as zero.\n116\t- **R0.7 The stable commands stay the judges.** `gnd extract`, `verify`,\n117\t  `check`, `fidelity` and `judgments check` are invoked as today. Neither\n118\t  example re-implements one of their checks or widens one of their caps.\n119\t- **R0.8 A parameter has a ceiling.** Each cap that becomes a parameter has a\n120\t  hard maximum fixed in its spec, and a time-bound test at that maximum\n121\t  (standing rule 2).\n122\t\n123\tThe project's six standing rules\n124\t(`.codex/skills/process-phase/references/project.md`) bind every phase; each\n125\tspec discharges them by name.\n126\t\n127\t## 1. Scope, sources and authority\n128\t\n129\tHarvested obligations. \"Observed\" means re-read or run on 2026-11-22 at\n130\t`4ad0d216`; \"inherited\" means claimed by a source and not re-measured;\n131\t\"proposed\" means new in this plan.\n132\t\n133\t| # | Source | Obligation | Kind | Phase |\n134\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n135\t| H1 | #216 \"Problem\" | `run()` (`reading.py:562`) builds `SavedProducer(inputs.responses)` at `:566`; only the private `_evaluate(..., producer=)` at `:445` takes another | observed | LH1 |\n136\t| H2 | `reading.py`, `ReadingInputs` | the root is bound to saved responses by the pin (`:72-75`, \"saved producer table differs from its pin\") and by the roster check (`:113`, \"saved response roster differs\"), and to the packaged vocabulary (`:135`) | observed | LH1 |\n137\t| H3 | `reading.py`, stage loop | the fixture bindings: the outline's six, the producer label and, found by the panel, the calibration path's literals `(p1)`: the `unavailable` scenario names `study-methods` (`:448`); `_calibrate` names that section, the fact `approximation` (`:317`) and `CALIBRATION_FACTS` (`:42`, `:311-327`), and the absence floor is the literal `[2, 2]` (`:372`); and the calibration path also fixes its renderer label `authored-public-fixture` (`:300`), its case folders `run-methods` and `held-back-predicate` (`:320`, `:323`) and its count of two cases (`:335`) `(p1)`; `_combined` adds `LABEL` to every population (`:48`, `:247`); `_public_results` needs `conflict.json`, `conflict.txt`, a `subject_label` fact with exactly two labels and exits 1 from `verify` and `check` (`:395-407`); `absence_status` needs the predicate `alternative_integrator` (`:373`); `_review_attempts` needs a `reviews.json` receipt bound to each accepted response's facts digest (`:198-209`); the extraction sidecar names its producer `saved-public-fixture` (`:264`) | observed | LH1 |\n138\t| H4 | `reading.py`, producer boundary | `produce(stage, section)` gets two names and no source bytes (`:156`); the loop reads a `calls` attribute from the producer (`:457`). `_inspect` accepts exactly three outcomes, `accepted`, `empty`, `refused` (`:185`). The outline's \"and the existing others\" was wrong: the other five coverage states are decided by the loop before or without a call | observed | LH1 |\n139\t| H5 | `depth.py:21-30`, `:56`, `:61` | three fixture-scale limits the outline did not list: every input file, source documents included, is read with a 128 KiB cap (`INPUT_LIMIT`); every JSON input is capped at 1 MiB; every `gnd` route has 30 seconds and 1 MiB of output (`PROCESS_LIMITS`). A caller document over 128 KiB refuses at read. Replay reads the run's own files through the same `_read` (`reading.py:414-437`), and each `payload-N.json` embeds every response of its stage (`:190-192`), so two 64 KiB responses already make a payload replay cannot read `(p1)` | observed | LH1 (D4) |\n140\t| H6 | `reading.py:27-30`, `:86`, `:263`, `:298`, `:384-387` | file names are fixed: the eleven `INPUTS` are all required; a source's path must be `<id>.txt`; the model must be `reading.orm.json` with one include named `vocabulary.orm.json`; the artifact names `reading.orm.projection.schema.json` and `reading.orm.applicable.json` are literals. Probe: `gnd extract caller.orm.json` on a renamed copy exits 0 and names its artifacts `caller.orm.*` | observed | LH1 |\n141\t| H7 | `reading.py:386`; include-root-routes IR2 | the loop runs `gnd extract` on the reading model after the producer has answered. `gnd extract` refuses a temporal model. A caller's reading model must be one `gnd extract` accepts, and a refusal there should cost no producer call | observed | LH1 |\n142\t| H8 | `reading.py:473-478`, `:172-174` | the global \"stop\" is a reason computed after the loop; the only thing that ends reading early is the byte budget, which omits sections. \"Per document\" has no present meaning | observed | LH1 (D6) |\n143\t| H9 | #216 \"Request\" | producer protocol, live and replayable; stop rule per document; roster cap and byte budget as parameters; `replay()` against a caller root | inherited | LH1, LH2 |\n144\t| H10 | consumers of `reading.py` | `revision.py:171` calls `reading.replay(retained, fixture=inputs, vocabulary_fixture=inputs)` and `:62-64` reads `reading.FIXTURE` and `reading.DEPTH_FIXTURE`; `assurance_report.py:760` calls `replay(run_dir)` with no root and `:725` caps coverage rows at 128 `(p1)`; the tests patch `SavedProducer.produce`, `_read` (on the replay path, `test_ld3_reading_workflow.py:238-246`) and `_public_results`, call `_inspect` directly with six positional arguments and read `producer.calls` (`:153-165`) `(p1)`, and read `ROSTER_LIMIT` | observed | LH1, LH2 |\n145\t| H11 | probe, 2026-11-22 | no test pins the bytes of a packaged reading run. `fidelity-high.json` and `fidelity-low.json` carry `gnd_version`. Evidence digests at gnd 0.4.0: `full` `ee67e13c…fd1c`, `--scenario unavailable` `d4010f94…6ddf`, `--budget-bytes 0` `7dcf43ec…a071` | observed | LH1 |\n146\t| H12 | #215 \"Problem\" | `project()` (`assertion_admission.py:100`) accepts three fact types only (`:105`); every role is a mandatory non-empty string (`:45-52`); absence is a sentinel (`:30`, `:84`); identity is (work, passage) (`:55-63`, `:111-113`); the admissible pair is hard-coded (`:122`); `MAX_TARGETS` (`:29`) bounds rows (`:46`) and targets (`:67`) | observed | LH3, LH4 |\n147\t| H13 | `src/gnd/judgments.py:115`, `:121` | `gnd judgments check` itself refuses a policy with more than 1,024 targets or more than 4,096 judge-target pairs. A `MAX_TARGETS` parameter can lower the example's bound; it cannot admit more than 1,024 targets in one call. The reporter's gold set has about 3,600 claims | observed | LH4 (D11) |\n148\t| H14 | `src/gnd/model/_validation.py:942`, `model_verbalization.py:124`, `evolve/service.py:577` | `objectified_as` is read by validation, verbalization and evolve only. The checker does not join an objectified fact's row to the entity that objectifies it; that link is the model author's convention | observed | LH3 (D10) |\n149\t| H15 | `assertion_admission.py:146-148`, `:188-191`; include-root-routes plan §1 H11 | the admission snapshot copies one model file, so an include root fails its include read. `gnd judgments check` accepts include roots since IR1 | observed | LH3 |\n150\t| H16 | `assertion_admission.py:35-39`, `:83-88`, `:118`, `:132` | checks that belong to the packaged shape only: the five closed tag vocabularies, the rule that `conditional` needs a condition and `unqualified` forbids one, and the fixed `covers` list | observed | LH3 |\n151\t| H17 | consumer of `assertion_admission.py` | `lineage_run.py:33` imports `ABSENT_CONDITIONS`, `PASSAGE`, `ROLES`, `SCHEMA`, `STATEMENT`, `WORK` and `admit`; `:203` calls `admit` with six positional arguments; `:254` compares the marker's `schema` with `SCHEMA` | observed | LH3, LH4 |\n152\t| H18 | probe, 2026-11-22 | packaged admission output at `4ad0d216`: `admitted.population.json` `6e45b93d…39e1`, `raw.population.json` `ea96584c…0173`, `judgment-report.json` `3726a424…c43c`, `admission.json` `6d262018…f16f`. Statement indices 3, 4, 5 and 6 pass `semantic-support` and fail `positive-own-findings` with `negated`, `baseline-comparison`, `speculation`, `cites-other-work` | observed | LH3, LH4 |\n153\t| H19 | #215, measured shape | 98 % of claims share a passage; about half have other than one actor and one object; 9 % have several conditions; 44 % carry a quantity | inherited | LH3 fixture |\n154\t| H20 | literature-depth close, row \"Host-scale reading and extraction\" | interface half only; the corpus and provider-budget run stays with the literature-identity follow-up | inherited | LH1, LH2 |\n155\t| H21 | PROJECT.md, roadmap after 0.4.0 | third milestone; independent of the two export milestones, both merged | inherited | all |\n156\t| H22 | component index, `scripts/components_index.py` | `examples.literature_reading` and `examples.literature_assertion_admission` exist, both `canonical`; the capabilities below adapt them and add no component. `review` lists seven literature entries whose descriptions are older than their code. The index changes in the same commit as the code | observed | all |\n157\t| H23 | probe, 2026-11-22; `reading.py:283-285`, `:393` | the loop completes only when `verify` and `check` both exit 0 on the broad and the targeted population, and admits only exits 0 and 1 from them. On a copy of the reading model, a population with one `system` fact and no `subject_label` exits 2 (`inadequate`) from both; adding one `subject_label` row gives 0 and 0. `LABEL` is what keeps the packaged model's one constrained fact type populated. A caller model with a constraint over a fact type a pass has not read yet exits 2, and the run refuses \"check exited 2\" | observed | LH1 (D16) |\n158\t| H24 | `docs/reference/rule-check-report.md:85-93`; `checker/_core.py:423`; `reading.py:268-271` | a checking route's exits are 0, 1, 2 and 4, with precedence 1 > 4 > 2 > 0; 4 means a must-hold rule is unchecked. `_route` refuses any exit outside the set it is given `(p1)` | observed | LH1 (D16) |\n159\t| H25 | probe, 2026-11-23; `project.md` lint gate | the whole-repository `uv run ruff check` exited 1 at `4ad0d216` with 198 findings, all in `review_tool.py`, `rules_check.py` and `handoff.py` as delivered from dev-tools (both discovery roots); `project.md` requires it to exit 0. Fixed upstream (dev-tools PR 34, `a29562a`) and re-synced; it exits 0 after the sync `(p1)` | observed | all |\n160\t\n161\tOut of scope: the corpus and provider-budget run (H20); promoting either\n162\tmodule to stable CLI; any change to a stable command or its caps (R0.7, H13);\n163\tthe assurance scorer's own 128-row cap (H10); #218, #221 and #222; release,\n164\ttag and publication.\n165\t\n166\t### Issue requests and the phases that discharge them\n167\t\n168\t| Request | Phase |\n169\t|---|---|\n170\t| #216: a public producer protocol, live and replayable | LH1, LH2 |\n171\t| #216: the stop rule per document as well as global | LH1 |\n172\t| #216: the roster cap and the byte budget as parameters | LH1 |\n173\t| #216: `replay()` against a caller-given input root | LH2 |\n174\t| #215: caller-named assertion entity, identity from its preferred uniqueness | LH3 |\n175\t| #215: every fact row of an admitted assertion kept | LH3 |\n176\t| #215: optional roles are optional | LH3 |\n177\t| #215: polarity and attribution rules as data, with a distinct disposition | LH4 |\n178\t| #215: `MAX_TARGETS` as a parameter | LH4 |\n179\t\n180\tThis is a framework milestone. An experiment-claim census (`gnd coverage`)\n181\tcounts claims over a domain model; it says nothing about which arguments an\n182\texample function accepts, so none is read here. The baseline is the test run\n183\tabove and the probes H6, H11, H18 and H23.\n184\t\n185\t## 2. Contract proposal and product behavior\n186\t\n187\tExact names and signatures are fixed in each phase spec. What follows is the\n188\tcontract each spec must realise.\n189\t\n190\t### LH1 — a live producer over a caller root\n191\t\n192\t- **The protocol.** A producer is any object with\n193\t  `produce(call) -> dict`. `call` is a `ProducerCall`: the stage, the section\n194\t  ID, its source ID and version, its kind and span, the section's source bytes\n195\t  and their sha256, and the open questions that point at the section. The\n196\t  result is the response object the loop accepts today: `outcome` (one of\n197\t  `accepted`, `empty`, `refused`), `facts` and `proposal_keys`. The loop\n198\t  counts calls itself; a producer needs no `calls` attribute. `SavedProducer`\n199\t  is one implementation and keeps its pin and roster checks.\n200\t- **A response is untrusted input.** The loop validates each response as a\n201\t  bounded JSON value of the declared shape before it uses it (standing\n202\t  rule 1). A response that fails, or a producer that raises, refuses the run\n203\t  with nothing written (D7).\n204\t- **A caller root.** A root that holds `profile.json` is a caller root. The\n205\t  profile names the reading model file and its include files, and supplies\n206\t  what the loop takes from constants today: the facts added to every\n207\t  population, the producer's label, and the optional review, calibration,\n208\t  conflict and absence steps. A\n209\t  root without a profile is the packaged layout and keeps the packaged\n210\t  constants. A live producer over a root without a profile refuses. A\n211\t  declared calibration step takes its renderer label and the names of its two\n212\t  case folders from the profile; the packaged label, folder names and count\n213\t  of two are the packaged profile's values (H3) `(p1)`.\n214\t- **Unavailable sections `(p1)`.** On a caller root the caller names its\n215\t  unavailable sections with an argument, and the manifest records them.\n216\t  `scenario=\"unavailable\"` names the packaged section `study-methods`, so\n217\t  over a caller root it refuses before any producer call.\n218\t- **Steps.** A step the profile does not declare is recorded `skipped`\n219\t  (R0.6). A declared step runs on the caller's files. A reviewer, if given,\n220\t  is called once per accepted response and must return a `supported` receipt;\n221\t  any other decision refuses the run, as a stale review does today. The\n222\t  reviewer is a check, not a filter (D3).\n223\t- **Order.** Inputs are validated, then the reading model is extracted, then\n224\t  the first producer call is made (H7, standing rule 3).\n225\t- **Route exits.** On a caller root the exits of `verify` and `check` over\n226\t  the broad and the targeted population are recorded as observed, 0, 1, 2 or\n227\t  4, and the run completes; any other exit refuses (D16, H23, H24) `(p1)`.\n228\t  The report shows the exits; it never calls a population verified. The\n229\t  packaged root keeps requiring 0.\n230\t- **Per document.** `document_budget` bounds the bytes read from each source.\n231\t  It follows the global budget's rule `(p1)`: a section that does not fit in\n232\t  what is left of its document's budget is omitted, and a later, smaller\n233\t  section of the same document is still read (`reading.py:172-174`). Other\n234\t  documents continue. The report states a stop reason for each source, with\n235\t  the three reasons the global stop has. A document whose required questions\n236\t  are resolved still gets its other scheduled reads (D6, ruled `(o)`). It is\n237\t  a `run()` argument beside the global budget, with the largest-budget limit\n238\t  as its ceiling (D4).\n239\t- **Limits.** The roster cap, the largest budget, the per-file input cap, the\n240\t  response cap, the route timeout, the route output cap and the retained-file\n241\t  cap are fields of one limits argument, each with a default equal to\n242\t  today's constant or today's effective bound (the retained-file cap of a\n243\t  `/2` run excepted, below), and a hard ceiling (D4, R0.8) `(p1)`. A JSON file is decoded under the cap it was read with, so a raised\n244\t  input cap also raises the decode cap of that input; the fixed 1 MiB decode\n245\t  cap of `depth.py:61` no longer applies to the reading loop.\n246\t- **Retained files `(p1)`.** The packaged `/1` run keeps today's path: no\n247\t  bound before the first call, and replay reads its files through\n248\t  `reading._read(folder, name)` as today, so its bytes do not move (R0.2)\n249\t  `(p2)`. A `/2` run, before its first producer call, computes an upper bound\n250\t  for each file it will retain from the roster alone: a file that carries\n251\t  responses or receipts holds at most the sections eligible for it times the\n252\t  response cap plus a per-attempt overhead the spec fixes; any other file is\n253\t  bounded by the roster cap times a per-row size the spec fixes `(p2)`. With\n254\t  no retained-file cap given, the run's cap is the largest of those bounds,\n255\t  and never less than 128 KiB; the run refuses if that exceeds the\n256\t  retained-file ceiling, or if the bounds exceed a cap the caller gave\n257\t  `(p2)`. The manifest records the cap, and replay of a `/2` run reads under\n258\t  it through a reader local to `reading.py` `(p2)`. A run that completes can\n259\t  therefore always be read back.\n260\t- **What a caller run writes.** The captured response table and the captured\n261\t  review receipts are artifacts of the run. Its manifest has format\n262\t  `literature-reading-run/2` and records the settings, the unavailable\n263\t  sections and each step's status (D5, R0.5). A packaged run with no new\n264\t  argument writes `literature-reading-run/1`, byte for byte (R0.2).\n265\t- In LH1, `replay()` refuses a `/2` run by name; LH2 replaces that refusal.\n266\t\n267\t### LH2 — replay of a live run\n268\t\n269\t- `replay(folder, fixture=ROOT)` reads the manifest, takes the settings and\n270\t  step statuses from it, reads the caller root through its profile, reads\n271\t  the run's files under the recorded retained-file cap `(p1)`, and\n272\t  re-derives every artifact. It has no producer, reviewer, budget or limits\n273\t  argument.\n274\t- It refuses a response table, receipt table, stage, payload or input whose\n275\t  bytes differ from what the manifest binds, and a manifest whose settings\n276\t  exceed a hard ceiling.\n277\t- A manifest binds a run internally. `replay(..., expected_evidence_sha256=)`\n278\t  compares the run with the digest `run()` returned, which the host keeps\n279\t  outside the folder; without it a consistently rewritten folder replays. The\n280\t  README says so. For the packaged run the independent producer pin keeps\n281\t  that role.\n282\t- `score_reading_coverage` gains the same `fixture` argument and passes it\n283\t  through. Its 128-row cap stays (out of scope).\n284\t\n285\t### LH3 — admission over a caller's assertion entity\n286\t\n287\t- **Shape.** The caller passes an assertion shape: the ID of an entity type\n288\t  in its model, and the entity types to follow from it (D17, ruled). With no shape,\n289\t  `admit()` runs the packaged shape and writes today's bytes.\n290\t- **Attached facts.** A fact type is attached when one of its roles is played\n291\t  by the assertion entity. A row belongs to the assertion its role value\n292\t  names. Every attached row of an admitted assertion is kept, except a row\n293\t  that also names an assertion that was not admitted: the one limit stated\n294\t  under \"Followed facts\", which holds for attached and followed rows alike\n295\t  `(p2)`.\n296\t- **Followed facts `(o)`.** Admission follows links outward (D9, ruled). When\n297\t  a kept row names an instance of a followed entity type, the rows of the\n298\t  fact types that entity plays a role in are kept for that instance too, and\n299\t  the walk continues from them. Example: a claim's row names quantity `Q7`;\n300\t  `Q7`'s value row and unit row are kept. The walk stops at the shape's\n301\t  list (D17, ruled): an instance of an entity type the shape does not list is\n302\t  kept as a value in the row that names it, and its own facts are not copied.\n303\t  With an empty list nothing is followed. One limit holds beside the list: a\n304\t  row that names an assertion which was not admitted is never kept, whichever\n305\t  route reaches it.\n306\t- **Coverage.** The admitted population's `covers` lists the attached fact\n307\t  types and the fact types of the followed entity types. A fact type outside\n308\t  both is not copied.\n309\t- **Identity `(o)`.** The assertion's own handle is its identity (D10,\n310\t  ruled). The model's preferred uniqueness is enforced by the stable\n311\t  commands, not re-implemented (R0.7). No rule links an objectified fact\n312\t  type's rows to the entity (H14): a shape that names an entity which\n313\t  objectifies a fact type refuses, naming the objectification. The packaged\n314\t  model's `assertion` is such an entity, so the packaged example stays on the\n315\t  packaged path.\n316\t- **Judged unit.** Policy targets are rows attached directly to the\n317\t  assertion. An assertion is supported when at least one of its rows is a\n318\t  target and every target among its rows is admitted by\n319\t  `gnd judgments check` (D8). A target on any other row refuses.\n320\t- **Optional facts.** An assertion with no row in an attached fact type\n321\t  simply has none. No value is read as \"absent\". The tag vocabularies and the\n322\t  condition rule of H16 apply to the packaged shape only.\n323\t- **Row bound `(p1)`.** The generic projection bounds the rows it reads,\n324\t  attached and followed together, with its own `max_rows` argument, separate\n325\t  from the target cap (D18). The packaged projection keeps `MAX_TARGETS` as\n326\t  its row bound (`:46`), so its bytes do not move. `max_rows` without a\n327\t  shape refuses the call, since the packaged projection would not apply it\n328\t  `(p2)`.\n329\t- **Include roots.** The snapshot copies each declared include beside the\n330\t  root, and the record binds each include's sha256 (H15).\n331\t- **Record.** A call with any new argument (a shape, rules, `max_targets` or,\n332\t  with a shape, `max_rows`) writes `literature-assertion-admission/2`; the packaged call\n333\t  with none keeps `/1` (D12). A `/2` record binds the shape and the rules by\n334\t  sha256 of their canonical bytes, and records `max_targets` and `max_rows`\n335\t  `(p1)`. A call with rules and no shape is a packaged-shape `/2` call: it\n336\t  records the shape as `packaged` and `max_rows` as `null` `(p2)`.\n337\t\n338\t### LH4 — admission rules as data\n339\t\n340\t- **Rules.** The caller passes admission rules: an ordered list of\n341\t  requirements, each with an ID, an attached fact type, a role, the\n342\t  admissible values and, optionally, a reason for each inadmissible value.\n343\t  An assertion satisfies a requirement when it has at least one row of that\n344\t  fact type and every such row has an admissible value. Requirements are\n345\t  evaluated in order, and the first one that fails gives the reason: the\n346\t  reason declared for the offending value, else the requirement's ID `(p1)`.\n347\t- **Default rules `(p1)`.** The packaged comparison at `:122` becomes the\n348\t  default rules, evaluated by the one evaluator (R0.4): first `polarity` with\n349\t  `asserts` admissible and the reason `negated` for `negates`, then\n350\t  `attribution` with `own-finding` admissible and each other value as its own\n351\t  reason. List order keeps today's polarity-first precedence, and the\n352\t  reasons are today's strings, so the H18 stage table does not move.\n353\t- **Disposition.** In a `/2` record, a supported assertion that fails a\n354\t  requirement has status `policy-refused` and that reason; an unsupported\n355\t  one keeps `fail` (D2). The `/1` record keeps today's statuses, so it keeps\n356\t  its bytes.\n357\t- **A rule that names nothing refuses.** A requirement naming an unknown fact\n358\t  type or role refuses the call. It is never evaluated as \"no row matched\".\n359\t- **The packaged shape `(p2)`.** There the assertion entity plays no role in\n360\t  the fact type that carries polarity and attribution (its roles,\n361\t  `assertion_admission.py:31-34`, have no `assertion`), so \"attached\" does\n362\t  not apply. On the packaged shape the one fact type a requirement may\n363\t  name is `assertion_statement` (`STATEMENT`, `assertion_admission.py:21`),\n364\t  which has one row per assertion, and the default rules name it. A\n365\t  requirement naming `assertion_names_work`, `assertion_names_passage` or any\n366\t  other fact type refuses there, as an unknown fact type does.\n367\t- **Target cap.** `max_targets` is an argument from 1 to 1,024. The ceiling\n368\t  is the stable command's (H13, D11).\n369\t\n370\t## 3. Dependencies and artifact homes\n371\t\n372\t| Phase | Needs | Homes it changes |\n373\t|---|---|---|\n374\t| LH1 | nothing | `src/gnd/examples/literature/reading.py`, `src/gnd/examples/literature/depth.py` (one keyword on `_command` and one on `_decode`, each defaulting to today's value `(p2)`), a new `tests/test_lh1_reading_host.py`, a new `tests/fixtures/lh1-reading/` (the caller root and the recorded packaged trees), `src/gnd/examples/literature/reading_fixture/README.md`, `dev/components/` |\n375\t| LH2 | LH1 | `reading.py`, `src/gnd/examples/literature/assurance_report.py` (one pass-through argument), a new `tests/test_lh2_reading_replay.py`, the same README, `docs/literature/commands.md`, `docs/release-notes.md`, `dev/components/` |\n376\t| LH3 | nothing | `src/gnd/examples/literature/assertion_admission.py`, a new `tests/test_lh3_admission_shape.py`, a new `tests/fixtures/lh3-admission/`, `src/gnd/examples/literature/README.md`, `dev/components/` |\n377\t| LH4 | LH3 | `assertion_admission.py`, a new `tests/test_lh4_admission_rules.py`, `tests/fixtures/lh3-admission/` (rules files), the same README, `docs/literature/commands.md`, `docs/release-notes.md`, `dev/components/` |\n378\t\n379\tOrder: LH1, LH2, LH3, LH4, one at a time on the branch\n380\t`claude/literature-host-interfaces`. The graph is acyclic. The two pairs share\n381\tno source file; they run in sequence so that `docs/literature/commands.md`,\n382\t`docs/release-notes.md` and `dev/components/` have one writer at a time. Start\n383\tcondition `(p2)`: `main` at the merge that lands this plan with the dev-tools\n384\tre-sync (H25). Its history holds `4ad0d216`, and so IR1, so LH3's fixture can\n385\tbe an include root. The LH1 spec records that commit and that the\n386\twhole-repository `uv run ruff check` exits 0 on it.\n387\t\n388\tFixtures are test fixtures under `tests/fixtures/`, not packaged examples: a\n389\tpackaged model would join the inventory in `tests/test_mc1_guards.py:365-376`\n390\tand the wheel. Each phase authors its own; no phase needs another phase's\n391\toutput files.\n392\t\n393\t## 4. Phases\n394\t\n395\t| Phase | Criteria | Inherited residuals |\n396\t|---|---|---|\n397\t| LH1 | **LH1-C1** a test producer with no `calls` attribute drives six stages over the caller root (two sources, its own model and include, its own section and fact names, no `responses.json`, `producer-pin.json` or `reviews.json`); broad and targeted populations exit 0 from `verify` and `check`; no packaged section, fact or label name occurs in the run folder. **LH1-C2** each call's bytes equal the section's span and its sha256 equals the attempt row's; `producer_calls` equals the calls the test producer counted. **LH1-C3** the run folder holds the captured response table and the captured receipts, both bound by the manifest. **LH1-C4** with no step declared, each step's status is `skipped` and the report says `skipped`, not `unavailable` and not a number; with each step declared, it runs on the caller's files; a declared file that is missing refuses before any producer call; unavailable sections named by argument are recorded, and `scenario=\"unavailable\"` over a caller root refuses before any producer call `(p1)`. **LH1-C5** a document budget ends one document and not the other; in the document it ends, a section after an oversized one is still read `(p1)`; each source has its stop reason; a document whose required questions are resolved early still gets its other scheduled reads (D6) `(p1)`. **LH1-C6** a lowered roster cap and budget ceiling are honoured and recorded; a raised input cap admits a 200 KiB source the default refuses; a limit above its ceiling refuses. **LH1-C7** each malformed response in the spec's list refuses with `ExampleError` and nothing written. **LH1-C8** a producer that raises refuses, naming the stage and section. **LH1-C9** the three packaged run configurations equal the trees recorded at `4ad0d216`, through the version helper `(p1)`; `test_ld3`, `test_ld4` and `test_ld5` pass unedited. **LH1-C10** each limit at its ceiling stays within the spec's time bound: inputs at each roster ceiling load, with the input cap raised as they need; a source at the input-cap ceiling, a response at the response-cap ceiling and a budget at its ceiling each run; a route that reaches the timeout refuses within the timeout plus its grace; a route whose output reaches the route-output ceiling, and a `/2` run that retains a file at the retained-file ceiling, each complete within the bound `(p2)`. The spec may let ceilings share one test if it says which and why `(p1)`. **LH1-C11** a model `gnd extract` refuses, and a live producer over a root with no profile, each refuse before any producer call. **LH1-C12** `replay()` of a `/2` run exits 3. **LH1-C13** a caller run whose broad population leaves a constrained fact type empty completes and records exit 2 for it; one whose targeted population violates a constraint completes and records exit 1; exit 4 is recorded like 2, held by a caller model whose check exits 4 or, if the spec shows no caller model can reach it, by a unit test of the exit table `(p1)`. **LH1-C14** `(p1)` `(p2)` a `/2` run whose bounds exceed a retained-file cap the caller gave refuses before any producer call; so does one whose derived cap would exceed the retained-file ceiling, held by a run at the roster and response ceilings with every section eligible; a run at the response ceiling whose eligible sections keep the bound within the ceiling completes, and each retained file reads back under the recorded cap; the LH1-C1 run, with default limits and more than one eligible section per stage, completes under the derived cap | H20 stays carried |\n398\t| LH2 | **LH2-C1** the LH1-C1 run replays from its folder and the caller root, byte-identical, with the same evidence digest and zero calls. **LH2-C2** one changed byte in the response table or in a receipt refuses; a consistently rewritten folder refuses when the expected digest is given. **LH2-C3** a run recorded with non-default limits replays with them, including the completing LH1-C14 run at the response ceiling `(p1)` `(p2)`; a manifest with a setting above its ceiling refuses. **LH2-C4** a changed source, profile, model or include refuses \"stale reading input\". **LH2-C5** a `/1` run replays as today; `test_ld3`, `test_ld4`, `test_ld5` pass unedited. **LH2-C6** `score_reading_coverage` scores the caller run when given its root. **LH2-C7** a manifest that claims `skipped` for a step the profile declares refuses | none |\n399\t| LH3 | **LH3-C1** a model with claims that share a passage, a claim with two actors and no object, one with three conditions and one with a quantity that is an entity with a value row and a unit row `(o)` admits; every attached row of each admitted claim is kept, with the rows of its quantity `(o)`, and no row of another claim is; `gnd check` on the admitted population exits 0. **LH3-C2** `(o)` a shape naming an entity that objectifies a fact type, the packaged `assertion` for one, exits 3 with a reason that names the objectification, and nothing is published. **LH3-C3** a claim with one admitted and one rejected target is not admitted; a claim with one admitted target and untargeted rows is, with all its rows; a claim with no target has disposition `missing`. **LH3-C4** a row attached to two assertions is kept only when both are admitted, the one exception to LH3-C1's \"every attached row\" `(p2)`. **LH3-C5** the model as an include root admits and the record binds each include; an include edited without a new pin exits 3 with nothing published. **LH3-C6** each refusal in the spec's list exits 3 with a value-free reason and nothing published. **LH3-C7** the value `none` in a generic-shape row is an ordinary value. **LH3-C8** the packaged call writes `admitted.population.json` and `raw.population.json` with the H18 digests and the H18 stage table; `test_li4` and `test_li5` pass unedited. **LH3-C9** 1,024 targets with attached and followed rows up to the `max_rows` ceiling project within the spec's time bound; one row over `max_rows` refuses `(p1)`; `max_rows` with no shape exits 3 with nothing published `(p2)`. **LH3-C10** `(o)` a quantity named by an admitted and by a rejected claim keeps its own rows once, and the rejected claim's row that names it is not kept; a row reached through a followed entity that names a rejected claim is not kept; an entity type the shape does not list is not followed (D17) | H15 closes here |\n400\t| LH4 | **LH4-C1** two rules files over the same inputs admit two different sets, each stated in the fixture. **LH4-C2** a supported assertion that fails a requirement has status `policy-refused`; an unsupported one has `fail`. **LH4-C3** an assertion with one admissible and one inadmissible row of the named fact type is `policy-refused`; so is one with no row of it. **LH4-C4** a lowered `max_targets` refuses a policy over it; a value above 1,024 refuses the call. **LH4-C5** a requirement naming an unknown fact type or role, a packaged-shape requirement naming `assertion_names_work` `(p2)`, and each malformed rules document in the spec's list, exit 3 with nothing published. **LH4-C6** the packaged call keeps the H18 bytes and statuses, now through the default rules and the one evaluator `(p1)`. **LH4-C7** `(p1)` a `/2` record binds the shape and rules digests and records `max_targets` and `max_rows`; changing any one changes the record; a call with rules and no shape writes `/2` with the shape `packaged` and `max_rows` `null` `(p2)` | none |\n401\t\n402\tEach phase also runs the structure and inventory guards, the\n403\twhole-repository `uv run ruff check` `(p1)`, an independent review of its\n404\tdiff, a refactoring review and its record.\n405\t\n406\t## 5. Documentation, skill and compatibility surfaces\n407\t\n408\t- **One home each.** The reading protocol, the profile, the limits and the\n409\t  `/2` manifest are described in\n410\t  `src/gnd/examples/literature/reading_fixture/README.md`, which already\n411\t  holds the stage files and the coverage states. The assertion shape, the\n412\t  admission rules and the `/2` record are described in the admission section\n413\t  of `src/gnd/examples/literature/README.md`. `docs/literature/commands.md`\n414\t  gains one short \"drive it from your host\" passage per example that links to\n415\t  those two homes and restates nothing normative.\n416\t- **Release notes.** One entry per pair under the unreleased heading of\n417\t  `docs/release-notes.md`, written by LH2 and LH4.\n418\t- **Skills.** No packaged skill names these functions; none changes.\n419\t- **Compatibility.** `SavedProducer.produce` takes one call object where it\n420\t  took two names; `test_ld3` patches it with a function that accepts any\n421\t  arguments. `test_ld3` also calls `_inspect` directly with six positional\n422\t  arguments and reads `SavedProducer.calls`, and patches `reading._read` on\n423\t  the replay path (H10). These stay: `_inspect` keeps its six positional\n424\t  parameters and its return shape, and every new parameter is keyword-only;\n425\t  `SavedProducer` keeps its `calls` counter, though the loop no longer needs\n426\t  one; `reading._read(folder, name)` stays the `/1` replay read path with its\n427\t  two positional parameters `(p1)`, and only a `/2` replay reads through the\n428\t  capped reader local to `reading.py` `(p2)`. `run()`, `replay()`, `admit()` and\n429\t  `project()` keep their present arguments and defaults. `/1` readers are not\n430\t  given `/2` documents by any packaged route.\n431\t- **Issue replies.** The close drafts a reply to #216 and to #215. Each says\n432\t  what was not done: the 1,024-target ceiling (H13), the corpus run (H20) and\n433\t  admission of an objectified fact type (D10).\n434\t  The owner posts them.\n435\t\n436\t## 6. Risks and required controls\n437\t\n438\t| Risk | Control |\n439\t|---|---|\n440\t| The generic projection admits rows the packaged example refused | LH3-C8 and LH4-C6 pin the packaged admitted set, the stage table and the output digests |\n441\t| Dropping the saved-response pin for live runs weakens the packaged replay | the pin and roster checks stay for `SavedProducer` (existing `test_ld3` cases); LH2-C2 binds a live run by digest |\n442\t| A caller root silently gets a packaged constant | the profile is the only source of caller bindings; LH1-C1 searches the run folder for packaged names |\n443\t| A skipped step reads as a passed one | R0.6; LH1-C4 and LH2-C7 |\n444\t| A provider answer crashes the loop after spend | LH1-C7, LH1-C8; the order rule and LH1-C11 put every input and model refusal before the first call |\n445\t| A completed caller run is read as a verified extraction | D16; the report carries the route exits, the README says a completed run is not a verified one, and LH1-C13 holds a run that completes with exits 2 and 1 |\n446\t| Following outward keeps a rejected assertion's row, or grows to most of the population | the limit in §2 \"Followed facts\" and D17; LH3-C10 holds a shared entity, a row reached the long way round and an unlisted entity type |\n447\t| The reporter's assertion is an objectified fact type after all | LH3-C2 refuses it by name, and the #215 reply states the ruling (D10), so the mismatch is reported, not silent |\n448\t| A raised cap turns a legal input into minutes of CPU | R0.8; LH1-C10, LH3-C9 |\n449\t| A live run retains files that its own replay cannot read `(p1)` | a `/2` run bounds its files before the first call and records its cap; LH1-C14, LH2-C3 |\n450\t| The retained-file bound refuses runs that work today `(p2)` | the bound applies to `/2` runs only, and a `/2` run's default cap is derived from its own bounds; LH1-C9, LH1-C14 |\n451\t| The generic admission path drifts from the packaged one `(p1)` | R0.4 names the shared homes; LH4-C6 runs the packaged rules through the one evaluator |\n452\t| A rule quantified \"any\" or \"every\" passes on fixtures that cannot tell them apart | LH3-C3, LH3-C4 and LH4-C3 each hold the discriminating case |\n453\t| A host reads \"parameter\" as \"no limit\" for `MAX_TARGETS` | LH4-C4; the README and the #215 reply state the stable ceiling |\n454\t| A large caller vocabulary exceeds the route timeout ([#221](https://github.com/vasiloglou/gnd-public/issues/221)) | the timeout is a limit (D4); a route that times out refuses naming the route |\n455\t| LH1 is larger than one phase | its spec states the size; if the estimate passes 400 source lines the spec proposes a split at its gate, with new IDs LH1a and LH1b |\n456\t| The packaged bytes pin moves at a version bump | the comparison uses `tests/support/recorded_version.py`, with `RECORDED_AT = \"0.4.0\"` |\n457\t\n458\t## 7. Out of scope\n459\t\n460\tThe corpus and provider-budget run; stable-CLI promotion of either module; a\n461\tchange to `gnd judgments check`'s caps or to any stable command; batching\n462\tinside `admit()`; the assurance scorer's row cap; admission of an entity that\n463\tobjectifies a fact type under a shape (D10, ruled); following an entity type\n464\tthe shape does not list (D17, ruled); #218, #221, #222;\n465\trelease, tag and publication.\n466\t\n467\t## 8. Execution, approval and review protocol\n468\t\n469\t- The plan review is held to the project's bar, the maintained GND review\n470\t  panel. The owner selected the full panel (D14, ruled `(o)` 2026-11-23); its\n471\t  first round read r4 and, at the owner's request, its second round read r5.\n472\t  The convergence rules allow two rounds at most, so r6 goes to the plan gate\n473\t  without a third `(p2)`.\n474\t- The plan gate is recorded in `001-plan-gate.md` once the owner rules, with\n475\t  the review's instrument, reviewed digest and dispositions, or the statement\n476\t  that none ran.\n477\t- Execution is `process-milestone literature-host-interfaces`, or\n478\t  `process-phase LH1 literature-host-interfaces` for one phase. `auto=true`\n479\t  needs the owner's explicit delegation for this milestone; none has been\n480\t  given (D15).\n481\t- Each phase: spec, spec gate, code, focused gates, one independent reviewer\n482\t  over the diff (D13), refactoring review, record, commit.\n483\t- Two pull requests (close A2): the reading pair (LH1, LH2) from\n484\t  `claude/literature-host-interfaces`, then the admission pair (LH3, LH4)\n485\t  from `claude/literature-host-interfaces-admission`, each to `main`. Before\n486\t  it, `python3 scripts/components_index.py changed --base origin/main\n487\t  --strict` exits 0. Merge stays with the owner.\n488\t\n489\t## 9. Per-phase verification\n490\t\n491\t| Phase | Focused lane | Expected |\n492\t|---|---|---|\n493\t| LH1 | `uv run pytest -q tests/test_lh1_reading_host.py tests/test_ld3_reading_workflow.py tests/test_ld4_revision.py tests/test_ld5_verdict.py tests/test_li1_assurance_report.py --tb=short` | exit 0 |\n494\t| LH2 | `uv run pytest -q tests/test_lh2_reading_replay.py tests/test_lh1_reading_host.py tests/test_ld3_reading_workflow.py tests/test_ld4_revision.py tests/test_ld5_verdict.py tests/test_li1_assurance_report.py --tb=short`, then `uv run python tools/run_tutorials.py --group literature` | exit 0 each |\n495\t| LH3 | `uv run pytest -q tests/test_lh3_admission_shape.py tests/test_li4_assertions.py tests/test_li5_lineage.py --tb=short` | exit 0 |\n496\t| LH4 | `uv run pytest -q tests/test_lh4_admission_rules.py tests/test_lh3_admission_shape.py tests/test_li4_assertions.py tests/test_li5_lineage.py --tb=short`, then `uv run python tools/run_tutorials.py --group literature` | exit 0 each |\n497\t| all | the structure and inventory guard block of `project.md`, and the whole-repository `uv run ruff check` `(p1)` | exit 0 |\n498\t\n499\tAffected consumers named here are read at each spec and may be widened there.\n500\tNo broad lane (`-m \"evidence or census\"`) is selected.\n501\t\n502\t## 10. Owner decisions and open questions\n503\t\n504\tD6, D9 and D10 carry the owner's ruling of 2026-11-22 `(o)`, and D14 and D17\n505\tthe rulings of 2026-11-23 `(o)`. Every other row is a proposal until the plan\n506\tgate rules it. D1–D3 keep their outline IDs; D4–D16 are new in r2, D17 in r3\n507\tand D18 in r5. r6 adds none.\n508\t\n509\t| ID | Question | Proposed | What the other reading costs |\n510\t|---|---|---|---|\n511\t| D1 | Where the producer protocol lives | In `reading.py`, until a second example uses it | A shared module now is a public home with one user |\n512\t| D2 | Name and reach of the LH4 disposition | `policy-refused`, in the `/2` record only; `/1` keeps `fail` | Applying it to `/1` changes the packaged record's bytes and `test_li4`'s expectations |\n513\t| D3 | The fixture-only steps on a caller root | Calibration, the conflict check and the absence scope are optional and declared in the profile; an undeclared step is `skipped`. The population label is profile data. A reviewer is optional, and a decision other than `supported` refuses the run | A reviewer that filters instead needs a ninth coverage state, which changes the row format #216 asks to keep |\n514\t| D4 | Which caps become parameters, and their ceilings | Seven limits `(p1)`: roster cap (default 128, ceiling 4,096), largest budget (1 MiB, 64 MiB), per-file input (128 KiB, 8 MiB), response (128 KiB, today's effective bound, since a response cannot exceed the 128 KiB read of `responses.json`; ceiling 1 MiB), route timeout (30 s, 600 s), route output (1 MiB, 16 MiB), retained file (for `/2` runs only `(p2)`: derived from the run's own bounds and never less than 128 KiB, today's read of a run's own files; ceiling 10 MiB, the repository's JSON read cap `gnd.input_policy.MAX_BYTES` (close A1)). The ceilings cannot all be reached together: at the 1 MiB response ceiling a retained file holds fewer than 10 eligible sections `(p2)` (close A1). A JSON file is decoded under its read cap. `document_budget` is a `run()` argument whose ceiling is the largest-budget limit | #216 names the first two only; with only those, a source over 128 KiB still refuses, a slow `gnd check` still stops the run at 30 s, and a live run can retain files its replay cannot read (H5) |\n515\t| D5 | Manifest format of a caller run | `literature-reading-run/2` for any run that uses a new argument; `/1` unchanged otherwise | Optional keys in `/1` let an older reader accept a run it cannot re-derive |\n516\t| D6 | What \"the stop rule per document\" means | **Ruled `(o)` 2026-11-22: as proposed.** A byte budget per source, enforced in the loop, and a stop reason per source in the report. A document whose required questions are resolved still gets its other scheduled reads | Ending a document at required coverage reads less, and changes which optional questions are answered; it could be a later switch |\n517\t| D7 | A malformed response, or a producer that raises | Refuse the run with nothing written; a host that prefers to continue returns `refused` from its own producer | Recording it as `refused` reports a transport failure as the producer's verdict |\n518\t| D8 | LH3: what is judged, and how row verdicts make an assertion's verdict | Targets are attached rows; supported means at least one target and every target admitted | \"Any target admitted\" admits an assertion with a rejected participant row |\n519\t| D9 | LH3: how far \"hangs off\" reaches | **Ruled `(o)` 2026-11-22: the other reading, follow entities outward.** Not adopted: directly attached fact types only; they form `covers`; other fact types are not copied | Following entities outward keeps a quantity entity's own facts, at the price of a reachability rule the model does not state. That rule is D17 |\n520\t| D10 | LH3: linking an objectified fact's row to its handle | **Ruled `(o)` 2026-11-22: the other reading, own handle only.** Not adopted: a link through the preferred external uniqueness, each identifying role matched to a role of the same name and object type in the objectified fact type | No rule means the objectified form is refused and only entities with their own handle are admitted, which leaves the packaged model on the old path only |\n521\t| D11 | LH4: the ceiling of `max_targets` | 1,024, the stable command's cap; a larger set is admitted in several calls the host makes | Raising `judgments.py`'s caps changes a stable command; that would be its own issue |\n522\t| D12 | LH3: the record of a shaped call | `literature-assertion-admission/2`; the packaged call keeps `/1` | One format with optional keys makes `lineage_run.py:254` accept records it was not written for |\n523\t| D13 | Phase diff reviewer | The review helper (`.claude/skills/process-phase/scripts/review_tool.py`, subject `phase`), one reviewer in a different tool | none; this is `project.md`'s default |\n524\t| D14 | Plan-review instrument | **Ruled `(o)` 2026-11-23: the full GND panel** (\"full\"). Its first round read r4, its second r5 | none |\n525\t| D15 | How the milestone runs | The owner selects: phase by phase with an owner gate on each spec, or `process-milestone … auto`. Ruled at the plan gate as phase by phase; superseded on 2026-11-23 by the owner's `/process-milestone literature-host-interfaces auto` (close A3) | none |\n526\t| D16 | LH1: which `verify` and `check` exits let a caller run complete | 0, 1, 2 and 4 are recorded and the run completes; the host gates on them; any other exit refuses (H24) `(p1)` | Requiring 0, as the packaged root does, refuses every run over a model with a constraint on a fact type not yet read (H23). Refusing on 1 only loses every producer call of a run whose extraction violates a constraint, which is a finding the host wants kept |\n527\t| D17 | LH3: where following stops (opened by the D9 ruling) | **Ruled `(o)` 2026-11-23: as proposed.** The shape lists the entity types to follow, for example `quantity`; an instance of any other entity type is kept as a value in the row that names it, and its own facts are not copied | Not adopted: following every entity type. It needs no list, but one admitted claim then keeps its paper, the paper's authors, their other papers and so on: most of the population, whatever was judged. With an empty list the result is the reading D9 did not adopt |\n528\t| D18 | LH3: the generic projection's row bound `(p1)` | A `max_rows` argument, default 16,384, ceiling 65,536, bounding attached and followed rows together; separate from `max_targets`. The packaged projection keeps `MAX_TARGETS` as its row bound, and `max_rows` without a shape refuses `(p2)` | Reusing `max_targets` as the row bound refuses 1,024 claims with two actors each (H19) |\n529\t\n530\tThe owner ruled D6, D9, D10 and D17 without waiting for the reporter. The\n531\treply to #215 states the three LH3 rulings, so the reporter can say if their\n532\tmodel does not fit them: an assertion is identified by its own handle,\n533\tadmission follows outward from it, and the host lists the entity types it\n534\tfollows.\n535\t\n536\t## 11. Rulings before the gate\n537\t\n538\t### 2026-11-22, r3\n539\t\n540\tOwner rulings given in the planning session, in reply to the three questions\n541\tr2 marked for the reporter. The owner's words: \"d6: proposed\", \"d9:\n542\talternative\", \"d10: own number\". Prior wording is r2's.\n543\t\n544\t| Tag | Site | Prior wording (r2) | Now |\n545\t|---|---|---|---|\n546\t| o | §10 D6; §2 LH1 \"Per document\" | proposal, \"To confirm with the reporter\" | ruled as proposed; no criterion changes |\n547\t| o | §10 D9; Goal LH3; §2 LH3 \"Attached facts\" | \"The admitted population holds exactly the attached rows of admitted assertions, and its `covers` lists the attached fact types (D9).\" | \"Followed facts\" and \"Coverage\" in §2; LH3-C1 widened; LH3-C10 added; D17 opened |\n548\t| o | §10 D10; Goal LH3; §2 LH3 \"Identity\" | \"For an entity that objectifies a fact type, a row of that fact type is linked to its handle through the entity's preferred external uniqueness, role by role (D10, H14). A row that links to no handle or to two refuses.\" | own handle only; an objectifying entity refuses under a shape |\n549\t| o | §4 LH3-C2 | \"the packaged model under a shape naming `assertion` admits exactly the statements whose target is admitted, each with its two link rows\" | the refusal of an objectifying entity |\n550\t| o | §7 | \"facts reachable from an assertion only through another entity (D9)\" | removed; two items added for D10 and D17 |\n551\t\n552\t### 2026-11-23, r4\n553\t\n554\tOwner ruling on D17, the question the D9 ruling opened. Asked \"the host lists\n555\tthe types, or follow everything?\", with the list as the proposal, the owner\n556\tanswered \"your auggestion\", read here as \"your suggestion\": the proposal.\n557\tPrior wording is r3's.\n558\t\n559\t| Tag | Site | Prior wording (r3) | Now |\n560\t|---|---|---|---|\n561\t| o | §10 D17 | proposal, with \"follow every entity type\" as the other reading | ruled as proposed |\n562\t| o | §2 LH3 \"Followed facts\" | \"Where the walk stops is D17. One limit holds under every reading of D17\" | the walk stops at the shape's list; an empty list follows nothing |\n563\t| o | §7 | \"following an entity type the shape does not list (D17, if ruled as proposed)\" | \"(D17, ruled)\" |\n564\t\n565\tNo criterion changed: LH3-C10 already hol